WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.250
Return to the original eight
o’clock request and inputs one, two.

00:00:05.740 --> 00:00:08.950
Give the matching positions
weights ten and five.

00:00:09.380 --> 00:00:10.750
Add a bias of twenty.

00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:13.000
The inputs describe the hour,

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:16.830
while these three parameters
define the current prediction rule.

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:20.590
All parameter values are
chosen for arithmetic practice.

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:23.730
They are not learned demand
estimates from the dataset.

00:00:24.140 --> 00:00:28.510
We will keep this starting state fixed
when comparing two alternative targets,

00:00:29.060 --> 00:00:31.810
so the effect of the changed
outcome stays visible.

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:37.970
Take the first input, one,
and its matching weight, ten.

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:39.670
Their product is ten.

00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:43.490
Pair the second input,
two, with weight five.

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:45.670
That product is also ten.

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:48.390
Adding the two products gives twenty.

00:00:49.060 --> 00:00:51.830
This subtotal contains both
weighted contributions;

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:54.250
the bias still needs to be added.

00:00:56.483 --> 00:01:00.293
The complete prediction adds a
bias to the weighted subtotal.

00:01:00.903 --> 00:01:04.573
Here the subtotal is twenty,
and the bias is another twenty.

00:01:05.143 --> 00:01:07.493
The resulting estimate is forty rentals.

00:01:08.543 --> 00:01:13.533
These arithmetic contributions do not
establish separate causes of demand.

00:01:14.083 --> 00:01:18.973
The bias provides a common offset,
and the calculation needs no later target.

00:01:21.233 --> 00:01:25.603
The prediction function receives features,
weights and bias.

00:01:26.353 --> 00:01:30.913
The source checks that the vectors have
compatible nonempty shapes and finite

00:01:30.913 --> 00:01:31.843
values.

00:01:32.553 --> 00:01:33.923
It then pairs the entries,

00:01:34.433 --> 00:01:37.103
multiplies each pair
and sums the products.

00:01:38.173 --> 00:01:40.383
The final addition supplies the bias.

00:01:40.773 --> 00:01:44.583
This displayed excerpt
shortens the validation code,

00:01:44.773 --> 00:01:46.743
but preserves the executed arithmetic.

00:01:47.813 --> 00:01:51.043
The full file is linked directly
below the video in the notes.

00:01:51.633 --> 00:01:54.723
A real-valued output is a point estimate.

00:01:55.553 --> 00:01:59.603
It can be fractional even though an
observed number of rentals is an integer.

00:02:01.775 --> 00:02:04.825
Suppose the later observed
count is forty five.

00:02:05.655 --> 00:02:09.225
This target is illustrative,
just like the starting parameters.

00:02:09.935 --> 00:02:13.725
Use prediction minus target
as the residual convention.

00:02:14.335 --> 00:02:17.285
Forty minus forty five is negative five.

00:02:17.835 --> 00:02:20.125
The model is five rentals too low.

00:02:20.875 --> 00:02:25.595
Keeping this sign matters because the
update must move the prediction toward the

00:02:25.595 --> 00:02:26.225
target.

00:02:26.815 --> 00:02:30.875
Changing the residual convention in one
function while keeping the old derivative

00:02:30.875 --> 00:02:33.565
elsewhere would reverse that correction.

00:02:34.015 --> 00:02:38.165
The target is used after the forecast,
when we measure its error.

00:02:40.353 --> 00:02:44.143
Choose half squared error for
this one-observation calculation.

00:02:44.973 --> 00:02:47.343
Squaring negative five gives twenty five.

00:02:48.013 --> 00:02:51.143
Multiplying by one half
gives twelve point five.

00:02:51.533 --> 00:02:54.543
The half simplifies the
derivative we will use next.

00:02:55.173 --> 00:02:59.313
There is no averaging denominator to
add because this example contains one

00:02:59.313 --> 00:03:00.083
observation.

00:03:00.533 --> 00:03:03.663
This scalar has squared rental units.

00:03:04.033 --> 00:03:05.733
It is not an accuracy percentage,

00:03:05.733 --> 00:03:11.223
and it is not the project’s later primary
evaluation measure, mean absolute error.

00:03:11.693 --> 00:03:15.603
The loss describes the current error
size under a particular objective.

00:03:17.732 --> 00:03:22.842
Keep the inputs and parameters fixed,
but change only the target to thirty five.

00:03:23.552 --> 00:03:27.982
The forward function still returns forty,
because it never receives the target.

00:03:28.532 --> 00:03:30.662
The residual is now positive five,

00:03:30.792 --> 00:03:33.802
while half squared error
remains twelve point five.

00:03:34.452 --> 00:03:37.942
Equal loss values do not
mean equal update directions.

00:03:38.752 --> 00:03:41.862
One estimate is too low
and the other is too high.

00:03:42.672 --> 00:03:46.612
Retain the signed residual and the
dependence of the prediction on each

00:03:46.612 --> 00:03:47.242
parameter.

00:03:47.832 --> 00:03:51.622
The chain rule uses those relationships
to calculate the correction,

00:03:51.932 --> 00:03:53.262
which is the next step.

